    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 5yr CAGR (7.1%) is >30% below EPS 5yr CAGR (17.7%): EPS is lifted by buybacks (~3% of shares retired since FY2020), margin expansion and the FY2020 COVID trough base. Revenue CAGRs (7.1% 3yr / 10.3% 5yr, net-revenue basis) include the US Ecology acquisition (2022) and ~$1B/yr of tuck-ins; organic growth is ~4-5% (core price 5-6%, volume -1%). The DCF uses organic growth, not the selected equity CAGR, because M&A spend is not modelled.",
    "notes": "CAGRs FY2022->FY2025 (3yr) and FY2020->FY2025 (5yr). Revenue from RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax (net), not the CSV's gross `Revenues` column. Owner FCF = OCF - capex - SBC (FY25 SBC estimated $44M on the 9M run-rate). SBC is flat at ~$40M while FCF grew, so the owner-FCF CAGR is marginally ABOVE the reported one -- no hidden stock-comp funding of growth."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Republic compounds as a price-led oligopolist: 4-5% core price offsets -1% volume, organic revenue ~4-4.5% decaying to 3%, margins inch up 0.7pp on automation and the Polymer Centers, capex stays slightly above D&A. Tuck-in M&A continues but is assumed to earn its cost of capital, so it adds nothing to value here.",
    "bull": "Volume recovers alongside sticky 5%+ pricing; Environmental Solutions and Blue Polymers (food-grade rPET/HDPE with Ravago) grow faster than the core and command premium pricing from brand-owner recycled-content mandates; automation and EV fleet take EBITDA margin to ~33%. Organic growth 5-5.5% for four years.",
    "bear": "The pricing tailwind fades with inflation, municipal re-bids get competitive, PFAS regulation (leachate treatment, Superfund designation) and labour inflation take ~1.3pp off margin and push capex up, and cash taxes normalise as bonus-depreciation deferral unwinds. Organic growth settles at 2.5%."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "A wide-moat, low-variance compounder priced as one. The owner-FCF DCF on organic growth and a 20x terminal cap values the equity below the market, which pays ~27x EV/unlevered owner FCF -- i.e. the market capitalises both the moat at a lower discount rate than 7.5% and the expectation that ~$1B/yr of tuck-in M&A earns well above its cost of capital. Neither is in the house base case.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "PFAS: EPA hazardous-substance designation could add leachate-treatment opex and closure/post-closure cost industry-wide; not quantified in filings",
      "Terminal cap binds in base and bear (Gordon at 7.5%/3.0% is ~22.9x); value is sensitive to the cap and to WACC",
      "M&A excluded: if tuck-ins earn well above WACC (plausible -- RSG buys at 6-8x EBITDA and synergises), the model understates value",
      "Cash tax: persistence of bonus depreciation and solar tax-credit purchases could keep cash tax well below the 22% terminal rate",
      "Leverage creep: ~$14B debt and rising if M&A and buybacks continue to be debt-funded"
    ]
  },
